  "critique": {
    "axis": "relevance",
    "evidence": "The screen clearly establishes the intent-integrity threat: a valid payment signature can authenticate a transaction that no longer matches the user’s shopping intent. The paired intended-versus-recorded payment evidence, agent identity, retrieved-page manipulation, payee continuity, and explicit “AUTHENTIC ≠ INTENDED” conclusion make the agentic-commerce defense story immediately legible across all viewports. However, the thesis does not explicitly connect the scenario to Mastercard rails, authorization controls, or a quantified defense outcome.",
    "fix": "Add one concise line tying intent verification to Mastercard payment authorization and state the concrete control or outcome, such as blocking payment when signed intent and transaction semantics diverge.",
